---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import MethodProvenance from "../../components/MethodProvenance.astro";
const defaultCode = `f <- function(x) sin(x)
fp <- function(x) cos(x)  # optional reference derivative`;
---
<BaseLayout title="Finite Differences — CMNA" description="Approximate derivatives of arbitrary R functions and watch the finite-difference geometry change with h.">
<header class="page-head"><div class="shell"><p class="eyebrow">Differentiation · CMNA Laboratory</p><h1 class="page-title">A derivative from nearby function values.</h1><p class="lede">Move a finite distance away from x, measure the change in f, and turn the resulting secant slope into an approximation of the tangent.</p></div></header>
<section class="section"><div class="shell two-column"><article class="content"><h2>Step size is the whole game.</h2><p>A large <code>h</code> measures the wrong neighborhood. A very small <code>h</code> eventually runs into floating-point subtraction. The interesting numerical question is not simply “make h smaller,” but how the approximation behaves as h changes.</p></article><aside class="callout"><strong>Optional exact derivative</strong><p>Define <code>fp</code> if you know the derivative. The lab will draw the true tangent and show error. Delete <code>fp</code> and the numerical methods still work.</p></aside></div></section>
<section class="section lab-section"><div class="shell" data-diff-lab>
<div class="lab-heading"><div><p class="section-kicker">Bring your own function</p><h2 class="section-title">Watch the secants become a tangent.</h2><p class="lede">R evaluates your function while the laboratory halves h repeatedly and compares forward, symmetric, and Richardson estimates.</p></div><div class="lab-expression"><span>Runtime</span><code data-status>Preparing R…</code></div></div>
<div class="r-guided-grid"><section class="r-guided-editor"><div class="r-editor-label"><span>R</span><small>Define <code>f</code> and optionally <code>fp</code></small></div><textarea class="r-editor r-function-editor" data-r-code spellcheck="false">{defaultCode}</textarea><div class="r-guided-controls"><label>x<input type="number" value="1" step="any" data-x /></label><label>starting h<input type="number" value="0.4" min="0" step="any" data-h /></label></div><div class="r-run-row"><button class="button r-run" type="button" data-run disabled>Differentiate in R</button><button class="r-reset" type="button" data-reset>Reset R session</button></div></section><aside class="r-guided-primer"><h3>Keep shrinking h.</h3><p>The symmetric difference usually improves much faster than the one-sided difference. If you push h far enough, floating-point arithmetic eventually joins the experiment.</p></aside></div>
<div class="lab-summary" data-summary></div><div class="lab-plot-frame"><svg class="lab-plot" role="img" aria-label="Function with forward and symmetric finite-difference lines" data-plot></svg></div>
<div class="lab-stepper"><button class="button secondary-on-light" type="button" data-play disabled>Shrink h</button><label><span data-step-label>h</span><input type="range" min="0" max="1" value="0" data-step disabled /></label></div><p class="lab-status" data-step-detail></p>
<div class="lab-table-wrap"><table class="lab-table"><thead><tr><th>level</th><th>h</th><th>forward</th><th>symmetric</th><th>Richardson</th></tr></thead><tbody data-rows></tbody></table></div>
</div></section><script is:inline type="module" src="/js/finite-difference-lab.js"></script>
  <MethodProvenance
    book="§5.1.1 · Finite Differences · p. 126"
    functions={["findiff","symdiff","rdiff"]}
    sources={["R/findiff.R"]}
    teachingHref="/teaching/chapters/#chapter-5"
  />

</BaseLayout>
